    id: 'kyutei-tulip-mania',
    title: 'What Really Happened During Tulip Mania?',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Whenever the price of a share, a house, or a digital currency rises so quickly that it seems to lose all connection with reality, commentators are likely to mention the tulip mania of the seventeenth century, when, according to a familiar story, the inhabitants of the Dutch Republic became so obsessed with tulip bulbs that some paid the price of a house for a single flower and many were ruined when prices suddenly collapsed. The story has become one of the most frequently cited warnings about the dangers of financial speculation, yet historians who have examined the original records have begun to question how much of it is true.",
      "There is no doubt that tulips were highly valued in the Netherlands in the early seventeenth century. The flowers had been brought to Europe from the Ottoman Empire only a few decades earlier, and certain varieties with striking patterns of color on their petals were especially admired. These patterns, it was later discovered, were caused by a virus that also weakened the plants, which meant that the most desirable bulbs were rare and difficult to multiply. During the winter of 1636 to 1637, prices for some of these varieties rose to extraordinary levels before falling sharply in February 1637, after which many buyers refused to pay for bulbs they had agreed to purchase.",
      "Much of the popular image of what followed, however, comes not from records of the time but from a book published in 1841 by the Scottish writer Charles Mackay, who described a whole nation seized by madness and left in poverty when the bubble burst. Mackay relied heavily on moralizing pamphlets written soon after the collapse, many of which were intended to criticize greed rather than to report events accurately, and his vivid account was repeated so often over the following century and a half that it came to be accepted as historical fact.",
      "When the historian Anne Goldgar studied surviving contracts, court records, and letters from the period, she found little evidence of the widespread ruin that the traditional story describes. The trade in expensive bulbs appears to have been limited to a relatively small group of merchants and skilled craftsmen, most of whom were wealthy enough to absorb their losses, and many of the agreements made at the height of the boom were never actually honored, ( A ) relatively little money changed hands. She could not find a single case of someone who was clearly driven into bankruptcy by tulips alone. Her findings do not prove that nobody suffered, but they make the image of a nation reduced to poverty very difficult to defend.",
      "If the economic damage was limited, why did the episode come to seem so important? Goldgar suggests that the real shock was cultural rather than financial. Dutch society placed great value on trust, honesty, and the keeping of promises, and the sudden refusal of so many buyers to honor their contracts, ( B ) the realization that the value of a beautiful object could disappear overnight, seemed to threaten the moral foundations on which commercial life depended. The pamphlets that later shaped Mackay’s account reflected this anxiety more than they reflected the actual scale of the losses.",
      "None of this means that speculative bubbles are harmless or that the lessons drawn from them are entirely mistaken. It does suggest, however, that a story repeated often enough can acquire an authority that the evidence does not support, and that historical examples used to illustrate economic arguments deserve the same careful scrutiny as the arguments themselves. The tulip mania may tell us less about the madness of crowds than about the way in which societies create stories to make sense of events ( C ) disturb their sense of order.",
    ],
